CHAPTERS.push(
{
  id: "carth-dido", set: "carthage", act: "Foundation", title: "The Legend of Queen Elissa", era: "Traditional · 814 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "Carthage's founding story is Phoenician, tragic, and — like Rome's — not history. It is also the only origin story the city's enemies bothered to preserve.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Flight from Tyre",
      text: ["The tradition holds that **Elissa** — the Greeks and Romans called her **Dido** — was a princess of **Tyre**, the greatest Phoenician city, and married to Acherbas, a priest of Melqart and one of the richest men in it. Her brother **Pygmalion** held the throne, wanted the money, and had Acherbas murdered at the altar, then told his sister nothing.",
        "In the story she is warned by her husband's ghost, and what she does next is not grief but planning. She recruits the noblemen the king has also alienated, loads ships as if leaving on the king's business, and takes the treasure with her — and once at sea she throws overboard bags weighted to look like the money, telling the king's agents aboard that this is what awaits them if they return empty-handed. They come with her. She has made them accomplices before they have noticed.",
        "Landing on the North African coast, she asked the local ruler for as much land as could be covered by an oxhide. He found this funny and agreed. She had the hide cut into a single continuous thread thin enough to encircle an entire hill — the citadel Romans later called the **Byrsa**, from the Greek word for oxhide, which is either the origin of the story or the reason it was invented.",
        "Every beat of that is a story about cleverness rather than power, told about a woman with no army in a foreign country. It is worth noticing what kind of founder a trading city chose for itself, against the kind Rome chose."],
      key: "Tradition says Elissa (Dido) fled Tyre after her brother murdered her husband, and won Carthage's citadel hill by a trick with an oxhide." },
    { title: "The story Rome added",
      text: ["In the Phoenician-flavoured version she dies too, and for a reason that fits the rest of her: a Libyan king demands marriage and threatens war, and rather than betray her husband's memory or expose her city she asks for time, builds a pyre ostensibly to make offerings, and kills herself on it.",
        "The story everyone knows is a different one. The love affair with **Aeneas** is a Roman literary invention, given its lasting form by **Virgil** in the *Aeneid* around 19 BC — some eight centuries after Elissa is supposed to have lived, and more than a century after Carthage was destroyed. Aeneas, fleeing Troy to found the Roman line, is blown ashore at Carthage; Dido falls in love; the gods order him on; and she curses his descendants from the pyre, prophesying an avenger to rise from her bones. Every Roman reader knew that avenger's name was Hannibal.",
        "It is magnificent poetry, and it is doing political work in every line. It converts three brutal and contingent wars into destiny — something fated from the founding, rather than something Rome chose. And it makes the most dangerous enemy Rome ever faced into a woman destroyed by love for a Roman, which is a considerably more comfortable story than the one where she nearly won.",
        "The app classifies the whole tradition as **Traditional / Legendary**, and flags the Aeneas strand separately as later fiction, because the two have been fused for two thousand years and were not the same thing."],
      key: "The Dido and Aeneas romance is a Roman literary invention by Virgil, written centuries after Carthage fell." },
    { title: "What the ground says",
      text: ["Excavation puts the earliest material at Carthage in the late ninth or early eighth century BC. That is broadly consistent with the traditional date of 814, which is unusual — most founding legends do not survive contact with a trowel — and suggests a real memory somewhere underneath the oxhide.",
        "What comes out of the ground is unmistakably Phoenician: the pottery, the burial rites, the alphabet, and the gods **Baal Hammon** and **Tanit**. Carthage began as one station in a network that ran from Tyre and Sidon along the North African coast to Iberia — Phoenician settlement was rarely conquest, because the point was harbours, water and a market rather than territory.",
        "The thing that made Carthage different was an accident of the sixth century. **Tyre** was besieged by Nebuchadnezzar for thirteen years and eventually absorbed into successive eastern empires, and the western colonies were left without a metropolis. Carthage, the largest and best sited of them, inherited the whole network — and, unlike its mother city, it had a hinterland it could tax and farm.",
        "The daughter outlived the mother and became something the Phoenician cities had never been: not a trading post, but a power."],
      key: "Archaeology dates Carthage's founding to c. 800 BC; it outlived Tyre and inherited the Phoenician trade network." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Where does the story of Dido and Aeneas come from?", options: ["Contemporary Phoenician records", "Virgil's Aeneid, centuries later", "Carthaginian temple inscriptions", "Polybius"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It is Roman literature written after Carthage's destruction, not a Punic tradition — which is why the app separates it from the Elissa story." },
    { q: "What does archaeology say about the traditional founding date of 814 BC?", options: ["It is centuries too early", "It is broadly consistent with the evidence", "The site was uninhabited until 300 BC", "The site was Greek before it was Phoenician"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Unusually for a founding legend, the traditional date sits close to what the material record supports." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-place", set: "carthage", act: "Foundation", title: "The City and Its Neighbours", era: "c. 800 – 500 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Carthage's whole strategy came out of its geography. Look at where it sits and the empire explains itself.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The best address in the Mediterranean",
      text: ["Look at where Carthage sits and most of its history explains itself. It occupies a promontory on the Gulf of Tunis, at the point where the Mediterranean pinches to its narrowest: the channel between Africa and **Sicily** is under 150 kilometres across. Every ship moving between the eastern and western halves of the sea passed within reach of it, and in a world where cargo moved by sea because moving it by land was ruinous, that is the most valuable address in existence.",
        "The city dug itself a double harbour, the **cothon**, and it is worth describing because nothing else like it existed. A rectangular merchant basin opened, through a channel that could be closed with a chain, into a circular naval basin about three hundred metres across with an island command post at its centre. Around the ring were covered ship-sheds — perhaps two hundred and twenty of them — so that the war fleet sat under roofs, invisible from the sea and from the commercial harbour. Appian says a merchant could enter the trading basin and see nothing of the navy at all.",
        "Behind the city lay the **Cape Bon** peninsula and the Bagradas valley: irrigated orchards, olives, vines and grain, some of the best farmland in North Africa, worked intensively enough that a Carthaginian named **Mago** wrote a twenty-eight-book treatise on agriculture which the Roman Senate ordered translated into Latin after the city fell — the only Punic book Rome deliberately preserved.",
        "Trade, a hidden navy, and food, all on one site."],
      key: "Carthage controlled the Mediterranean's narrowest channel and built the cothon, a double harbour with a hidden naval basin." },
    { title: "Who else was there",
      text: ["Immediately inland were the **Libyans**, farming populations whom Carthage taxed at rates that reached a quarter and, in wartime, half the crop. They rose whenever Carthage looked weak, and the bitterness of that relationship is a recurring cause of Carthaginian defeats — an empire that squeezes its nearest subjects hardest has no depth to fall back on.",
        "Beyond them were the **Numidians**, kingdoms of the Massylii and Masaesyli whose light cavalry was the best in the western Mediterranean: no saddles, no bridles, javelins, and a way of fighting built on speed and feigned retreat. Carthage hired them for centuries. It never fully secured them, and the moment they changed sides — at Zama — it lost everything.",
        "Across the water was **Greek Sicily** and above all **Syracuse**, the rival of two hundred years. To the north the **Etruscans**, early allies against Greek expansion; then, further up the same coast, a small inland city called **Rome**, with which Carthage signed perfectly friendly commercial treaties from the sixth century BC onwards. There was nothing inevitable about what happened later; for four hundred years the two states got on.",
        "And west, past the Pillars of Hercules, lay the Atlantic — the route to Cornish tin and West African gold. Carthage guarded it with an aggression it showed nowhere else. **Strabo** records a Carthaginian captain who, finding himself shadowed by a Roman ship trying to learn the route, deliberately ran his own vessel aground to destroy them both, and was compensated out of the public treasury on his return."],
      key: "Carthage was ringed by Libyans, Numidian cavalry kingdoms, Greek Sicily, Etruscans and later Rome — and controlled the Atlantic trade route." },
    { title: "How it was run",
      text: ["After an early period of kingship Carthage became a republic, and a stable one. Two chief magistrates, the **suffetes**, were elected annually — the word is cognate with the Hebrew *shophet*, judge. Below them sat a senate of elders, a popular assembly with real powers when the senate and suffetes disagreed, and the **Council of 104**, which audited returning generals and had the authority to crucify the unsuccessful ones. Carthaginian commanders operated knowing that failure was not merely the end of a career.",
        "**Aristotle** thought the constitution good enough to analyse in the *Politics* alongside Sparta's and Crete's, and praised it for stability — no tyrants, no serious civil strife in centuries, which was more than any Greek city could say. His criticism was that it openly rewarded wealth: offices were effectively bought, and he thought a state that treats office as purchasable will end up governed by people who expect a return.",
        "The structural flaw was elsewhere, and it was military. Policy was made by merchant families who fought their wars with **mercenaries and subject levies** — Libyans, Numidians, Iberians, Gauls, Balearic slingers — rather than with citizens. It is an efficient system: you buy exactly the specialists you need, and your own men stay at their trades earning the money that pays for it.",
        "It fails in one specific way. A citizen army that loses goes home. A hired army that loses, and is not paid, is still armed and still in your country. Carthage discovered this after the First Punic War in the most direct manner imaginable."],
      key: "Carthage was a republic of elected suffetes and a merchant senate that fought with mercenaries rather than citizens." },
    { title: "The Tophet, and reading hostile sources",
      text: ["Greek and Roman writers accused Carthage of burning its own children alive as offerings to **Baal Hammon** and **Tanit**. Diodorus describes a bronze statue with sloping arms from which infants rolled into a fire pit, and says that in a crisis of 310 BC the nobility sacrificed five hundred children, having been caught substituting bought ones for their own.",
        "There is a site. The **Tophet** precinct at Carthage has yielded thousands of urns containing the cremated remains of infants and of young animals, each buried under a stone marked with a dedication to the two gods, in continuous use for six centuries.",
        "What it proves is genuinely **Contested**, and the argument is live among specialists rather than settled. One reading takes the sources broadly at their word: ritual child sacrifice, exceptional in the Mediterranean world but not unattested. The other reads it as a cemetery for infants who died of natural causes in a society with appalling infant mortality, given a religious dedication precisely because the loss was grievous — with the sacrifice story a slander that grew in the telling among people who were at war with Carthage for three hundred years. Skeletal, dental and isotopic studies have been published supporting both, and the presence of animal remains in the same urns has been read as evidence for both substitution and for ordinary votive practice.",
        "This app does not resolve it, and the reason for spending a whole part on it is not the sacrifice. It is the method. **Punic literature is gone.** After 146 BC the Roman Senate gave the libraries of Carthage away to Numidian kings, keeping only Mago's book on farming, and none of it survives. There is no Carthaginian history, no Carthaginian speech, no Carthaginian account of any war it fought. Almost every written word in this Set comes from Greeks and Romans who fought the city and, in the end, erased it.",
        "Every claim in these chapters carries that discount. Where the app can check the sources against the ground, it says so. Where it cannot, it says that too."],
      key: "Child sacrifice at the Tophet is genuinely contested; almost all surviving evidence about Carthage was written by its enemies." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What was the cothon?", options: ["A Carthaginian law code", "An artificial double harbour with a hidden naval basin", "The council of elders", "A type of warship"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The circular naval basin behind the merchant harbour let Carthage conceal the size and readiness of its fleet." },
    { q: "What was the structural weakness of Carthage's military system?", options: ["It had no navy", "It relied on mercenaries and subject levies rather than citizens", "Its generals were elected annually", "It banned cavalry"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Hired armies cannot absorb catastrophic losses the way a citizen levy can, and become dangerous when unpaid." },
    { q: "Why must claims about Carthage carry a discount?", options: ["The sources are undated", "Punic literature is largely lost, so the record comes from Carthage's enemies", "Carthaginians did not write", "All sources are medieval"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Source hostility is a systematic bias, not an occasional one — it shapes the entire surviving picture." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-sicily", set: "carthage", act: "Empire", title: "The Sicilian Wars", era: "580 – 265 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Three centuries of intermittent war with the Greeks of Sicily. Carthage never won it and never let it go.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Why Sicily",
      text: ["Sicily is the largest island in the Mediterranean, it grows grain in quantity, and it sits directly across the sea lane Carthage lived on. Whoever held all of it could close the channel.",
        "It had been settled from both ends. Greek colonists took the east and south from the eighth century BC — Syracuse, Acragas, Gela, Himera — and Phoenicians held the west from **Motya**, **Panormus** and **Lilybaeum**, ports that faced Africa and were a day's sail from it. In between were the Sicels and Elymians, whose own history is largely lost between two better-documented neighbours.",
        "Neither side could accept the other holding the whole island. Neither could take it. Carthage could always land an army and could never keep one supplied indefinitely against a hostile interior; Syracuse could beat a Carthaginian army and could never follow it to Africa and finish the job.",
        "So the argument ran for three hundred years — longer than the United States has existed — in bursts of extreme violence separated by treaties that drew a line across the island and then failed. It is the single longest-running war in the ancient Mediterranean, and it is almost forgotten, because both of the states that fought it were eventually destroyed by a third."],
      key: "Sicily's wealth and position made it a permanent, unresolvable contest between Carthage and the Greek cities." },
    { title: "Himera, and the century that followed",
      text: ["The decisive early defeat came at **Himera** in 480 BC, against Syracuse and Acragas. Herodotus says the Carthaginian commander **Hamilcar** spent the battle sacrificing at an altar, and when he saw his army breaking, threw himself into the fire.",
        "Greek tradition placed Himera on the same day as **Salamis**, and the coincidence did a great deal of work: a single civilisational moment in which Greeks in the east held off Persia and Greeks in the west held off Carthage, on one afternoon, by providence. That framing is an **Interpretation**, it is almost certainly a later construction, and it tells you a great deal about how Greeks wished to see themselves and nothing whatever about what Carthage thought it was doing — which was, on the evidence, protecting a trading position at the request of an allied Sicilian tyrant.",
        "The defeat kept Carthage out of Sicily for seventy years, and some historians read that pause as the moment it turned inland and outward instead: taxing the Libyan hinterland properly, and sending fleets down the Atlantic.",
        "When the war restarted in 409 BC it ran, on and off, for a century and a half: against **Dionysius I of Syracuse**, who besieged and destroyed Motya with catapults — a new weapon — and then lost most of what he had gained; against **Timoleon**; and against **Agathocles**, who in 310 BC found himself besieged in Syracuse and did the one thing nobody expected. He left the city under blockade, took his army by sea to **Africa**, and burned his own ships on the beach so that his men understood there was no way home. He ravaged Carthaginian territory for three years and very nearly took the city.",
        "He failed in the end, and abandoned his own army to do it. But he had proved the strategy worked, and a Roman would read about it."],
      key: "Defeat at Himera (480 BC) checked Carthage in Sicily for seventy years; Agathocles later invaded Africa in 310 BC." },
    { title: "Trade was the real prize",
      text: ["Underneath three centuries of fighting sat something less dramatic and more important. Carthage was not trying to build a Sicilian empire. It was protecting a monopoly.",
        "The treaties with Rome, which Polybius saw and quoted, are unusually revealing about how the system worked. The earliest specifies where Roman ships may and may not sail, requires that any trading they do in Carthaginian territory be conducted in the presence of a herald or notary, and flatly forbids them from going beyond a fixed cape at all. A later treaty bars Romans from trading in Sardinia and Libya entirely, and from founding anything in Sicily. This is not diplomacy between equals. It is a schedule of permissions.",
        "The wealth those permissions protected came from West African gold, Iberian silver, Sardinian grain, purple dye worth more by weight than silver, and above all the carrying trade — being the ship that everyone else's goods travelled in.",
        "So Carthage did not need to rule Sicily. It needed nobody else to rule Sicily. That is a lower bar and, for three hundred years, an achievable one.",
        "It became unachievable when a new kind of state arrived. When **Pyrrhus of Epirus** campaigned in Italy and Sicily in the 270s BC and briefly looked like unifying the island, Carthage and Rome fought on the same side — the last time they ever would. Pyrrhus, leaving Sicily, is said to have looked back and remarked what a fine wrestling ground they were leaving for Rome and Carthage."],
      key: "Carthage fought to keep the western Mediterranean closed to competitors; treaties with Rome restricted where Roman ships could trade." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why could neither Carthage nor the Greeks settle the Sicilian question?", options: ["Neither could conquer the whole island, and neither could accept the other holding it", "A treaty forbade fighting", "Rome intervened each time", "The island was too poor to fight over"], correct: 0,
      explain: "Structural deadlock, not lack of effort — which is why the wars recur across three centuries." },
    { q: "How does the app classify the tradition that Himera was fought on the same day as Salamis?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "Interpretation — it reflects Greek self-image", "Unknown"], correct: 2,
      explain: "The framing serves a Greek narrative of civilisational defence; it is a reading of the events, not a verified synchronism." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-hanno", set: "carthage", act: "Empire", title: "Hanno the Navigator", era: "c. 500 BC", minutes: 6,
  intro: "A Carthaginian fleet sails past the edge of the Greek map, and files a report we can still read.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The voyage",
      text: ["Around 500 BC a suffete named **Hanno** took a fleet out through the Pillars of Hercules and turned left, down the Atlantic coast of Africa. The stated purpose was colonisation — the account claims sixty ships and thirty thousand settlers, which is certainly inflated but indicates the scale intended — and it names the towns founded along what is now Morocco.",
        "The remarkable thing is that we can read his report. The ***Periplus of Hanno*** survives as a Greek translation of a Punic text said to have been set up in the temple of Baal at Carthage. It is a handful of pages, written in a flat first-person plural, and it is very nearly the only Carthaginian voice that has come down to us at all.",
        "What it describes gets stranger as it goes south. Coastal lagoons full of elephants. Crocodiles and hippopotami in a river. People in animal skins who threw stones at them. Then, at night, a coastline of fires and the sound of drums, pipes and shouting, which frightened the crews enough that they sailed on. Then a mountain streaming flame into the sea, which they called the **Chariot of the Gods**.",
        "And finally an island in a gulf, full of hairy people whom their Libyan interpreters called ***gorillai***. The women could not be caught; three were taken and were so violent that they had to be killed, and their skins were brought back to Carthage. In 1847 an American missionary borrowed that word from the *Periplus* to name a newly described ape, which is why the animal has a Punic name recorded by a Carthaginian admiral two and a half thousand years ago."],
      key: "Hanno the Navigator sailed the Atlantic coast of Africa c. 500 BC; his Periplus survives as a Greek translation." },
    { title: "How far, and how sure",
      text: ["How far he actually got is **Contested** and the range of serious opinion is wide: southern Morocco at the cautious end, Sierra Leone or even Gabon at the ambitious one. The identifications turn on matching described features — a volcano, a gulf, a river with crocodiles — to real coastline, and the further south you go the more the fit depends on which volcano you are prepared to accept.",
        "Some scholars go further and doubt the voyage as described altogether, reading the text as part genuine record and part promotional literature: a state advertising the reach of its navy, or discouraging others from following. The hairy islanders in particular may be chimpanzees, baboons, or a people described by frightened men through two layers of interpretation.",
        "What is not in dispute is the pattern the voyage belongs to. Carthage also sent **Himilco** north along the Atlantic face of Europe, toward Brittany and probably Britain, chasing tin — the ingredient without which there is no bronze. Fragments of that account survive too, describing a sea of weed, no wind, and sea-monsters, which reads like either genuine Sargasso-type observation or a deliberate attempt to make the route sound unusable to anyone else.",
        "Either way this is a state systematically exploring and settling ocean coastlines, with state funding and a written record, at a period when Greek geography still largely hugged the Mediterranean shore. It is the single clearest sign of what was lost when Carthage's libraries were dispersed."],
      key: "How far Hanno sailed is contested, but Carthage clearly ran systematic Atlantic exploration, including Himilco's northern voyage." },
    { title: "An empire of harbours",
      text: ["The Punic model of expansion was not conquest, and this is the thing most easily missed about Carthage because it is the opposite of how Rome worked. What Carthage planted was a chain of coastal footholds: a sheltered anchorage, a warehouse, a temple, a market, and an accommodation with whoever ruled the interior. It wanted the harbour, not the hinterland.",
        "That is cheap, it scales quickly, it requires very few of your own citizens, and it leaves almost nothing behind. No road network, no colonial cities laid out on a grid, no monumental inscriptions announcing the conquest of anybody. It is one reason Carthage is archaeologically thinner than its importance warrants, and why the popular picture of the ancient western Mediterranean is so lopsidedly Roman.",
        "By the mid-third century BC the network ran from the Atlantic coast of Morocco through southern Iberia, the Balearics, Sardinia and western Sicily. On paper Carthage was the dominant power in the western Mediterranean, richer than any Greek state and with a navy no one could match.",
        "Its weakness was the mirror of its strength. An empire of harbours has no depth. It can be unpicked one port at a time by anyone willing to spend the years, and it depends absolutely on controlling the sea between the links.",
        "In 264 BC Rome intervened in a squabble at Messana over a garrison, and began, without intending to, doing exactly that."],
      key: "Carthage expanded through chains of coastal trading footholds rather than territorial conquest." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What is the Periplus of Hanno?", options: ["A Roman forgery of the 1st century AD", "A Greek translation of a Punic account of an Atlantic voyage", "A Carthaginian law code", "A Greek epic poem"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It is one of very few Punic texts to survive in any form, and only because it passed through Greek." },
    { q: "What characterised the Punic colonial model?", options: ["Mass settlement of farmers inland", "Chains of coastal trading footholds with local arrangements", "Direct military occupation", "Religious conversion of neighbours"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Harbours and warehouses, not provinces — cheap and flexible, but archaeologically faint." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-after-first", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "What the First War Cost", era: "264 – 237 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "The war itself is in the Wars tab. This is what it did to Carthage — which is the part that causes the next one.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The war in one paragraph",
      text: ["In 264 BC Rome put a garrison into **Messana** in Sicily, and a local dispute between mercenaries became a war that ran for twenty-three years — the longest continuous war in ancient history, and by some measures the largest naval war ever fought before the modern era.",
        "Carthage began it with total command of the sea, three centuries of naval tradition, and an opponent with essentially no ships. It ended it with no fleet, no Sicily, and a debt.",
        "How that happened — the copied warship, the corvus, the fleets lost in storms, the final battle at the Aegates Islands — belongs to the **First Punic War** entry under Wars, which opens once you have studied both societies up to 264 BC. This Set does not tell it twice, and telling it from one side only would be worse than not telling it at all.",
        "What this chapter is about is the bill, because the bill is what causes the next war."],
      key: "The First Punic War (264–241 BC) cost Carthage Sicily, its naval dominance and a heavy indemnity." },
    { title: "The bill",
      text: ["The financial terms were 3,200 talents over ten years, on top of the direct cost of building and losing fleet after fleet. Sicily went, and its western half — held for something close to four hundred years — became Rome's first overseas province, taxed to pay for the next war.",
        "Look at what that does to a state built the way Carthage was. Its entire military system rested on hiring soldiers, and hiring soldiers rests on revenue. Carthage had just lost the revenue and acquired the debt in the same document. It now had to pay off an indemnity, pay its existing army, and rebuild — with the Sicilian ports gone and the sea no longer securely its own.",
        "The political damage was worse than the financial. **Hamilcar Barca** had held the mountains of western Sicily for years, was never beaten in the field, and was still holding when the government negotiated the peace over his head and instructed him to arrange the evacuation. He obeyed, and he resigned rather than surrender the arms himself.",
        "So the war left a state that had been defeated and a general who had not. Those two things do not sit comfortably in one country, and everything in the next fifty years of Carthaginian history follows from the gap between them."],
      key: "Carthage lost Sicily and took on an indemnity while its undefeated field commander came home to a peace he rejected." },
    { title: "Sardinia",
      text: ["The worst of it came three years later, in the middle of the mercenary revolt that is the subject of the next chapter.",
        "With Carthage fighting for its life inside its own territory, the Carthaginian garrison on **Sardinia** mutinied in sympathy and appealed to Rome. Rome had refused a similar appeal from Sardinia once already, correctly, as a violation of the treaty. This time it accepted. When Carthage began preparing to recover the island, Rome declared that these preparations were an act of war against Rome, and offered peace on terms: cede Sardinia and Corsica, and pay a further 1,200 talents.",
        "Carthage, with an enemy army in sight of its walls and no capacity to fight anyone else at all, agreed.",
        "**Polybius** is the crucial witness here. He is Greek, he wrote inside the household of the Roman general who destroyed Carthage, and he is not sentimental about Carthaginians anywhere in his history. On this he says plainly that the seizure was contrary to all justice, that Rome had no reasonable ground whatever, and that it was among the principal causes of the war that followed. When a source that partial concedes that your side was in the wrong, the concession is worth more than a hostile witness's accusation.",
        "The Carthaginians did not forget it. Neither, specifically, did the family of the general who had been in the field when the first peace was signed."],
      key: "Rome seized Sardinia and Corsica during Carthage's mercenary crisis; even Polybius calls it unjust and names it a cause of the next war." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why does the app put the battles of the First Punic War in a separate Wars entry?", options: ["They are unimportant", "So each side's own history stays about that society, not the fighting", "The sources are missing", "To reduce loading time"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Wars are shared events between two societies; the app treats them as their own subject rather than duplicating them in both syllabi." },
    { q: "What does Polybius say about Rome's seizure of Sardinia?", options: ["That it was lawful under treaty", "That it was contrary to justice and a cause of the next war", "That it never happened", "That Carthage requested it"], correct: 1,
      explain: "A pro-Roman source conceding Roman fault is unusually strong evidence, which is why it carries weight here." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-truceless", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "The Truceless War", era: "241 – 237 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "The most savage war Carthage ever fought was against its own army, and it very nearly ended the city.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Unpaid",
      text: ["The army came home from Sicily in 241 BC expecting years of arrears. Carthage was broke, and made a decision that reads as reasonable and turned out to be close to fatal: instead of paying, it moved the troops out of the city to a holding camp, kept them together, and opened negotiations to settle the debt at a discount.",
        "There were around twenty thousand of them and they had one thing in common, which was that Carthage owed them money. Otherwise they were Libyans, Iberians, Gauls, Ligurians, Greeks and Balearic islanders, with no shared language — Polybius notes that officers could not address the whole force in any single tongue, so every message had to pass through interpreters who could be, and were, suspected of shading it.",
        "The haggling convinced them they were being cheated, which they were. They mutinied under **Spendius**, an escaped Campanian slave who knew that any settlement would return him to a cross, and **Mathos**, a Libyan who knew the same about his own people.",
        "And then it stopped being a pay dispute. The revolt spread into the **Libyan** countryside, where Carthage had been taxing farmers at half the harvest to fund the war, and where the resentment was three generations deep. The Libyans supplied men, food and money — Polybius says even the women handed over their jewellery — and Carthage found itself facing a veteran army of perhaps seventy thousand in its own hinterland, with almost no citizen soldiers of its own, having spent four hundred years arranging never to need any."],
      key: "In 241 BC Carthage's unpaid mercenary army mutinied and drew in the Libyan countryside, threatening the city itself." },
    { title: "Why 'truceless'",
      text: ["What makes this war distinct is that both sides deliberately destroyed the possibility of ending it.",
        "The rebel leadership had a specific problem: their men could always be bought back, and Carthage was rich. Spendius solved it by arranging the murder of the hostages — the general **Gisco** and some seven hundred Carthaginians, whose hands were cut off and legs broken before they were thrown into a pit alive — and then announcing that any future envoy would be treated the same way. It was not rage. It was policy: after that, no man in the rebel army could negotiate, because Carthage could never forgive it.",
        "Carthage answered in kind, trampling prisoners under elephants and taking no captives thereafter.",
        "**Polybius** knew a great deal about ancient warfare and calls this the most savage and unprincipled war of which he had any knowledge. He also gives it the name it still carries — the war *without a herald*, without truce, because a herald was the mechanism by which ancient armies talked, and both sides had killed theirs.",
        "It is the sharpest available demonstration of what Carthage's system actually cost. Rome, going through the same postwar bankruptcy at the same moment, faced nothing remotely comparable — because Roman soldiers were citizens with farms, who could be sent home and made to wait for their money."],
      key: "Both sides made quarter impossible; Polybius called it the most savage war he knew and named it the Truceless War." },
    { title: "Hamilcar's rescue, and his conclusion",
      text: ["Carthage turned, eventually, to the man it had sidelined. **Hamilcar Barca** was given a command alongside a rival general, and the two of them disagreed until the army was invited to choose between them, which is not how the Council of 104 preferred to do things.",
        "He won the war by 237 BC, and he won it by manoeuvre. He was operating against men who had served under him in Sicily and who knew his methods, in country they knew as well as he did, and he beat them by getting the rebel armies into ground they could not leave — most famously in the place Polybius calls the Saw, a defile where a trapped force starved, ate its prisoners and then its slaves, and was destroyed when it finally came out. Spendius was crucified in sight of the city. Mathos was flogged through the streets of Carthage by the relatives of the men he had killed.",
        "Then Hamilcar drew his conclusions, and they were structural rather than personal. Carthage's government had lost a war it could have won, signed a peace over its general's head, failed to pay the army that peace released, and nearly been destroyed by the consequences. An army belonging to that government would always be underpaid at the worst possible moment.",
        "So the next army would not belong to it. It would be raised abroad, paid out of resources he controlled directly, commanded by his family, and situated far enough from the Council of 104 that the Council would be presented with results rather than consulted about plans.",
        "He looked west, at **Iberia** — silver, manpower, and two thousand kilometres between him and the Carthaginian senate. He took his nine-year-old son with him."],
      key: "Hamilcar ended the revolt in 237 BC and concluded that any future army must be funded and commanded independently of Carthage." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What caused the Truceless War?", options: ["A religious dispute", "Carthage delaying payment to its returning mercenary army", "A Roman invasion", "A Numidian succession crisis"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The mercenary system's basic failure mode: an army with no citizenship stake, and a state that could not pay it." },
    { q: "What lesson did Hamilcar take from the revolt?", options: ["That mercenaries should be replaced with citizens", "That any future army must be funded and commanded independently of Carthage's government", "That Carthage should disarm", "That Rome could be trusted"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It is the direct origin of the semi-private Barcid empire in Spain." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-spain", set: "carthage", act: "Decline", title: "The Conquest of Spain", era: "237 – 219 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "A defeated state builds a better empire than the one it lost — and hands it to a family rather than a government.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Iberia",
      text: ["In the spring of 237 BC **Hamilcar Barca** sacrificed before leaving Carthage for **Iberia**, and his nine-year-old son begged to be taken along. Polybius has the scene from Hannibal himself, told decades later to King Antiochus of Syria as an explanation of why he could never be trusted to make a lasting peace with Rome: his father led him to the altar, laid his hand on the victim, and made him swear that he would never be a friend to the Roman people. Then he took him.",
        "Hamilcar had spent the Truceless War hunting down the mercenaries he had once commanded, and he crossed to Spain with an army the state had not paid for and could not easily recall. He worked upward from **Gades**, the old Phoenician colony on the Atlantic, and over nine years took the south and east by a mixture of siege, pitched battle, marriage and hire. Iberian nobles came into his service with their warbands; their communities came into tribute. Where he could not conquer, he recruited.",
        "Underneath all of it was **silver**. The mines of the Sierra Morena and the hills behind modern Cartagena were among the richest deposits in the ancient world. Polybius went to look at them and described forty thousand men working the shafts near New Carthage and a daily yield to the treasury of twenty-five thousand drachmas. Slaves cut the galleries, water-screws drained them, and the metal left through the harbour in ingots.",
        "Hamilcar died in 228 BC, drowned in a river — Diodorus says while drawing an enemy force away from his sons, Livy simply says in battle. The accounts agree on nothing except that he did not come back.",
        "In nine years a defeated state had rebuilt its finances, its army and its fleet. It had done so through a project that belonged, in every practical sense, to one family."],
      key: "Hamilcar conquered southern Iberia from 237 BC; its silver rebuilt Carthaginian finances and funded a standing army." },
    { title: "New Carthage",
      text: ["Command passed to **Hasdrubal the Fair**, Hamilcar's son-in-law and a far better diplomat than soldier. Where Hamilcar had taken Iberia by campaigning, Hasdrubal took the rest of it by treaty and marriage — he married an Iberian princess himself, and the Iberians, according to Diodorus, acclaimed him as their supreme general, a title no Carthaginian magistrate had any business holding.",
        "In 227 BC he founded **Qart Hadasht**, New City, the same name Carthage itself carried. Romans called it **Carthago Nova**; it is Cartagena today. The site is one of the finest in the western Mediterranean: a deep bay screened by an island, a peninsula joined to the land by a bar that shows only at low water, hills that will carry a wall on every side. Into it Hasdrubal put a palace, a mint, arsenals, dockyards and the whole administration of the silver. It was a capital — and it was four hundred miles by sea from the council that was supposed to be running it.",
        "The coins say the same thing more quietly. Barcid silver struck in Spain carries a male head in the guise of Melqart, club at the shoulder, and the face changes as the commanders change. Carthage had never put a living man on its money.",
        "Hasdrubal was murdered in 221 BC inside his own palace, stabbed by a Celt whose master he had put to death. Livy says the man was taken and tortured and kept the same expression throughout, as though he had already got what he came for. The army in Spain did not wait for instructions from home. It acclaimed **Hannibal**, aged about twenty-five, and Carthage ratified what the army had decided.",
        "By then the Barcids held territory, revenue, a capital, a fleet and an army recruited locally and sworn personally. What they did not hold was a mandate. The council in Carthage could approve or disapprove; it could not recall."],
      key: "Hasdrubal founded Carthago Nova in 227 BC as a Barcid capital; Hannibal took command by acclamation in 221 BC." },
    { title: "The Ebro line and Saguntum",
      text: ["Rome watched the recovery with alarm and no free hands. The Gauls of the Po valley were massing for the invasion that ended at **Telamon** in 225 BC, and Illyrian piracy had already cost two wars. What Rome could afford was a line on a map. In about 226 BC its envoys and Hasdrubal agreed the **Ebro treaty**, conventionally read as fixing the river Ebro as the northern limit of Carthaginian expansion.",
        "**Saguntum** was the problem the treaty did not solve. The town lay well south of the Ebro, deep inside the zone Rome had just conceded, and it held an alliance with Rome. Whether that alliance was made before the treaty or after it was disputed in antiquity and cannot be settled now — and it is exactly the point on which the question of who broke faith turns.",
        "Hannibal besieged Saguntum in 219 BC. It held eight months, long enough for embassies to travel and achieve nothing. He was badly wounded at the wall by a javelin, and when the town finally fell Livy says some of its people burned their possessions, and themselves, rather than be taken.",
        "Rome demanded that Hannibal be surrendered. Livy stages the answer as theatre: the envoy Quintus Fabius gathered a fold of his toga and told the Carthaginian council that he carried peace and war in it and would let out whichever they chose; they told him to let out whichever he liked; he shook out war, and they said they accepted it. Whether or not anyone said those words, the war began in 218 BC.",
        "Responsibility is **Contested**, and was contested by the ancient writers themselves. Polybius names three causes: Hamilcar's unextinguished anger, the Barcid success in Spain that made a second war thinkable, and Rome's seizure of Sardinia during the Truceless War, which he calls contrary to all justice. He blames Barcid ambition and concedes the Carthaginian grievance in the same argument. Both can be true at once, and the treaty had been drafted loosely enough for each side to believe itself inside it."],
      key: "Hannibal's siege of Saguntum in 219 BC triggered the Second Punic War; responsibility for the treaty breach is genuinely contested." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What funded the Barcid recovery in Spain?", options: ["Roman subsidies", "Iberian silver mines", "Egyptian grain", "Sicilian tribute"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Silver paid the indemnity, the army and the fleet — a state-scale revenue stream controlled by one family." },
    { q: "Why was the status of Saguntum so contentious?", options: ["It was north of the Ebro", "It was Greek", "It lay inside the Carthaginian zone but held a Roman alliance", "It had no walls"], correct: 2,
      explain: "The timing of Rome's alliance relative to the treaty is exactly what decides who broke faith — and it cannot be settled." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-zama", set: "carthage", act: "Fall", title: "No Longer an Empire", era: "202 – 183 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Carthage survived the Second Punic War. It did not survive the peace terms.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The terms",
      text: ["The defeat at **Zama** in 202 BC ended the war; the battle itself belongs to the **Second Punic War** entry under Wars. What matters here is the settlement, which was designed less to punish Carthage than to make it permanently incapable.",
        "Carthage kept its African territory and lost everything else: Spain, the islands, every overseas possession. It handed over its elephants and undertook to train no more. It surrendered its navy down to ten triremes. Livy describes the rest — five hundred ships — being towed out past the harbour mouth and burned in full view of the city, and says the people watching from the walls mourned as though Carthage itself were alight. It accepted an indemnity of ten thousand talents payable over fifty years, and gave a hundred hostages against it.",
        "And it accepted one clause that mattered more than all the others together. Carthage would make no war outside Africa at all, and none inside Africa without Rome's permission.",
        "When the terms were read out at home a senator named Gisgo rose to argue against accepting them, and Hannibal — who had lost the battle and knew precisely what the alternative was — took hold of him and pulled him down off the platform. The assembly was scandalised. He apologised, saying he had left Carthage at nine years old and had learned a soldier's manners rather than a citizen's, and then told them to accept, and to be glad the terms were no worse.",
        "Rome had not annexed anything. It left a wealthy trading city standing with its constitution, its territory and its commerce intact, and removed only its capacity to defend them. A state that cannot go to war without another state's consent is a client, whatever the document calls it. The clause was not a humiliation to be lived down over time; it was a mechanism, and it would take fifty years to finish working."],
      key: "The 202 BC terms stripped Carthage of empire, navy and the right to wage war without Roman permission." },
    { title: "Masinissa's licence",
      text: ["Rome then made a king. **Masinissa** was a Numidian prince who had fought for Carthage in Spain, changed sides when the war turned, and brought the cavalry that broke Hannibal's flank at Zama. Rome confirmed him as king of a united Numidia along Carthage's landward border and guaranteed him. He was about fifty at Zama. He reigned for another half-century.",
        "That kingdom was Rome's other creation of 202 BC, and the more durable one: a single Numidia where there had been rival chiefdoms, with a capital at Cirta, minted coinage, grain for export and an army drilled on the Roman model. Rome had not merely left a neighbour on Carthage's border. It had built one.",
        "Masinissa spent his reign taking Carthaginian land, and the pattern never varied. Numidian settlement or raiding pushed into a border district; Carthage protested to Rome as the treaty obliged it to; a Roman commission came out to arbitrate; the commission found for Masinissa. The rich coastal Emporia went that way. So did the farmland of the Great Plains, and in the 150s BC some fifty or seventy towns. Cato himself came out on one of these commissions.",
        "Carthage could not fight, because fighting without permission broke the treaty. It could not win an arbitration, because the arbitrator had installed the other party. It could not appeal past Rome, because by now there was nobody else to appeal to.",
        "The permission clause had turned a border dispute into a legal trap. Resisting meant war with Rome; not resisting meant losing the country in instalments. Every year Carthage complied was a year it grew smaller, and the only exit from the trap led straight to the end."],
      key: "Rome guaranteed Masinissa's Numidia, which annexed Carthaginian land for fifty years under Roman arbitration." },
    { title: "Hannibal's second career",
      text: ["The unexpected part is what **Hannibal** did with his defeat. He neither fled nor retired onto his estates. In 196 BC, six years after Zama, he stood for office, was elected **suffete** — one of the two annual chief magistrates — and turned on his own governing class.",
        "The Council of 104, the judicial body that had shadowed and second-guessed Carthaginian generals for two centuries, held office for life and answered to nobody. Hannibal made it annually elected and barred consecutive terms. When a quaestor belonging to the judges' faction ignored a summons, Hannibal had him arrested, brought him before the popular assembly, and used the occasion to attack the entire order. Then he opened the accounts. The revenues of the state, he showed, were more than enough to pay Rome's indemnity without a single new tax. They were simply being stolen, in quantity, by the men complaining loudest about the burden.",
        "The reforms worked. Within a few years Carthage offered to settle the whole remaining fifty-year indemnity in one payment. Rome refused. A Carthage out of debt would be a Carthage out of reach.",
        "His enemies at home, whose incomes he had ended, wrote to Rome accusing him of conspiring with **Antiochus III** of Syria for a new war. A Roman commission arrived to investigate. Rather than let his city be forced to hand him over, Hannibal left Carthage at night, rode to a tower he kept on the coast and sailed for Tyre. He served Antiochus, then Crete, then **Prusias of Bithynia**, always useful and always a bargaining chip, until Rome asked for him one last time. At **Libyssa**, around 183 BC, with the house surrounded, he took the poison he had carried for years, remarking that the Roman people could not wait for an old man to die.",
        "He had beaten Rome in the field for sixteen years and was finished off by the accountants of his own city, who used Rome as the instrument. Carthage's ruling class preferred a diminished city it controlled to a strong one it did not — the same preference that would leave the city, thirty-seven years later, with nobody able to defend it."],
      key: "Hannibal reformed Carthage's finances as suffete, was denounced by his own oligarchy, and died in exile c. 183 BC." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Which peace term mattered most in the long run?", options: ["The loss of elephants", "The ban on waging war without Rome's permission", "The loss of Sicily", "The surrender of hostages"], correct: 1,
      explain: "It made self-defence against Masinissa legally impossible — the mechanism that eventually produced the Third Punic War." },
    { q: "Why did Rome refuse Carthage's offer to pay the indemnity early?", options: ["The sum was too small", "A debt-free Carthage would be an independent one", "Rome had lost the treaty", "Carthage lacked the money"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The indemnity was leverage as much as compensation, so early settlement was against Roman interests." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "carth-end", set: "carthage", act: "Fall", title: "Delenda Est", era: "149 – 146 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Carthage recovered commercially, complied legally, and was destroyed anyway.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The recovery that condemned it",
      text: ["By the 150s BC the indemnity was paid off and Carthage was visibly rich again. It had no empire, no navy and no wars. It had the harbour, the African hinterland, and fifty years of trade uninterrupted by the expense of defending anything.",
        "That was the problem. Roman envoys who went out to arbitrate Masinissa's latest encroachment came back unsettled by what they had seen: a full harbour, crowded streets, timber and iron in the yards, a countryside of orchards and estates. **Cato the Elder** had been on one of those embassies. Pliny reports that he came into the Senate afterwards, shook a fresh African fig out of a fold of his toga onto the floor, and asked the house how far away the country was that had grown it. Three days' sail, he told them.",
        "Thereafter, whatever the debate, Cato is said to have finished every speech he made by declaring that Carthage must be destroyed. The famous Latin phrase *Carthago delenda est* is a later formulation rather than a quotation, and is classified here as **Traditional / Legendary** in its wording; the campaign itself is **Established**.",
        "He did not have the Senate to himself. **Scipio Nasica** argued, repeatedly and for years, that Rome needed a rival to stay honest — that fear of Carthage was the discipline holding the Roman state together, and that removing it would turn Roman aggression inward. He lost the argument. Within two generations Rome was fighting itself."],
      key: "Carthage's commercial recovery alarmed Rome; Cato's campaign to destroy it is established, the famous phrasing is not." },
    { title: "The trap closes",
      text: ["In 149 BC Carthage finally did the forbidden thing. Masinissa's raids had run for half a century; Carthage raised an army under a general named Hasdrubal, marched out to stop him without asking Rome, and was beaten. It had now lost twice over — in the field, and in the legal cover it had spent fifty years protecting.",
        "Rome declared war and landed an army at Utica, which had already changed sides. Then the demands began, and they were designed to be met.",
        "First, three hundred hostages, children of the noble families, to be delivered at Lilybaeum. Carthage delivered them; the accounts dwell on the scenes at the harbour as the ships took them off. Next, all weapons and war material. Carthage handed over what is reported as two hundred thousand sets of armour and two thousand catapults, and the consuls praised the city for its obedience.",
        "Only then, with the hostages gone and the arsenals empty, did the consul **Censorinus** deliver the last demand. The Carthaginians were to leave their city and settle somewhere at least ten miles inland, away from the sea. The site would be destroyed; the people would not be harmed.",
        "For a nation whose whole existence — trade, revenue, religion, identity — ran through a harbour, that was not relocation but extinction, and the envoys understood it as such immediately. Rome had taken the compliance first and made the impossible demand last, which is the order you use when what you want is the refusal rather than the surrender."],
      key: "Rome extracted hostages and weapons, then demanded Carthage abandon its coastal city entirely; Carthage refused." },
    { title: "146 BC",
      text: ["Carthage, with no weapons and no allies, refused — and then held out for three years.",
        "The city turned itself into a workshop. Public buildings were stripped for metal and temples for timber, and the accounts claim a daily output of a hundred and forty shields, three hundred swords, five hundred spears and a thousand catapult bolts, with women cutting off their hair to make cordage for the torsion springs. The siege proper — the mole thrown across the harbour mouth, the blockade, the starvation — belongs to the **Third Punic War** entry under Wars.",
        "**Scipio Aemilianus**, adopted grandson of the Scipio who had beaten Hannibal, took the walls in the spring of 146 BC. The fighting went street by street and roof by roof for six days, up the terraces towards the citadel of Byrsa. The Carthaginian commander Hasdrubal came out and surrendered himself; his wife, watching from the temple roof with the last of the defenders, is said to have called down that he had bought his own life at the price of his city, and then killed her children and walked into the fire.",
        "Perhaps fifty thousand survivors were sold into slavery, and the site was methodically demolished. The story that Rome ploughed salt into the fields has **no ancient source at all** and appears to be a nineteenth-century invention.",
        "Polybius was standing beside Scipio while the city burned, and records that he wept and quoted Homer on the fall of Troy — not out of pity for Carthage, but because he had just understood that the same thing would one day be done to Rome. A century later Rome founded a colony on the same ground. It became one of the great cities of the empire and, in time, the see of Augustine. The place Rome erased is the place Rome rebuilt."],
      key: "Carthage fell in 146 BC after a three-year siege; the salting of the fields is a modern invention with no ancient source." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What was Rome's final demand before the war?", options: ["A larger indemnity", "The surrender of Hannibal", "That Carthage abandon its coastal city and move inland", "Conversion to Roman religion"], correct: 2,
      explain: "It was framed as a relocation and functioned as an extinction order for a maritime trading society." },
    { q: "How should 'Rome salted the fields of Carthage' be classified?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "A modern invention with no ancient source", "Traditional / Legendary from Polybius"], correct: 2,
      explain: "No ancient writer mentions it; the story enters the record in the nineteenth century." },
  ],
},
);
